--TEST--
The implicit widening into a `float` stops exactly at 2^53: the last whole number that fits goes through and the first one that does not throws, for `int` and `uint` alike — `rule:types/implicit-widening`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/widening-without-as
// The bound is named on both sides in one case on purpose. A widening that
// stopped one number early, or one number late, prints plausibly against
// either half alone.
int $last = 9007199254740992;
float $fits = $last;
echo $fits, "\n";

int $first = 9007199254740993;
try {
    float $over = $first;
    echo "widened ", $over, "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $e) {
    echo "int: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// The unsigned row is the same bound, and it is `uint`'s own diagnostic
// rather than a shared one that names `int`.
uint $ulast = 9007199254740992;
float $ufits = $ulast;
echo $ufits, "\n";

uint $ufirst = 9007199254740993;
try {
    float $uover = $ufirst;
    echo "widened ", $uover, "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $e) {
    echo "uint: ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// It is the same conversion a written `as float` performs, so the two
// spellings agree at the boundary rather than each having a bound of its own.
try {
    echo $first as float, "\n";
} catch (ArithmeticError $e) {
    echo "as: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
9.007199254741E+15
int: cannot convert `int` 9007199254740993 to `float`
9.007199254741E+15
uint: cannot convert `uint` 9007199254740993 to `float`
as: cannot convert `int` 9007199254740993 to `float`
